  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Thursday...\n\nHigh pressure building into the region from the west brought warm\nconditions to the area today. The ridge axis shifts east on \nSaturday, allowing southwest flow to develop over the region, as\nan upper low approaches the Pac NW. Moisture will increase out of\nthe south, with PWATs approaching 1.25-1.5 inches (150-200% of\nnormal). A weak shortwave lifting into the region will bring\nscattered showers and thunderstorms to the area during the\nafternoon and evening. There could be a couple strong storms, with\nstrong winds and small hail. With the high PWATs, any \nthunderstorm will be capable of producing heavy rainfall. \n\nThe upper low pushes east along the US/Canada border Sunday into \nMonday, breaking down the upper ridge. As the low slides east, an\nassociated cold front is expected to push through the area \nSunday. Areas west of Billings could see a period of elevated \nfire weather concerns on Sunday as pre-frontal conditions bring \nwesterly wind gusts of 15-30 mph, with humidity down near 20 \npercent. Scattered showers and thunderstorms look to spread across\nthe area during the afternoon and evening as well, with gusty \nwinds, small hail and heavy rainfall. Monday will see mainly dry\nconditions behind the cold front with temperatures a few degrees\ncooler. Upper ridging builds back over the area Tuesday into\nThursday, with weak impulses and moisture bringing periodic \nchances for showers/thunderstorms, greatest in the south. Yet \nanother break down of the upper ridge could occur by Friday.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to range from the 80s to lower 90s\nSaturday, to the 90s many places on Sunday. Temperatures then cool\nback into the 80s to near 90 degrees Monday through Thursday. STP",
